# PART 02 — ART DIRECTION: DEFINE A DISTINCTIVE PUBLICATION IDENTITY

## 2.1 The design ambition

The intended product should feel like a sophisticated international intelligence publication.

Its design language should combine:

- Editorial authority.
- Contemporary magazine art direction.
- Serious analytical presentation.
- Modern information design.
- High-end typography.
- Carefully controlled visual experimentation.
- Excellent digital usability.

Use publications and studios as design references, not templates to copy.

Study the visual principles of:

- The New York Times.
- Financial Times.
- The Economist.
- Reuters Graphics.
- Bloomberg.
- The Information.
- Monocle.
- Premium independent editorial publications.
- Award-winning digital editorial and information-design projects.

Do not reproduce their logos, layouts, or proprietary identity.

Find a distinctive design direction for WORLD SIGNAL itself.

## 2.2 Avoid the generic AI product aesthetic

The following patterns are explicitly discouraged:

- Generic SaaS dashboards.
- Repetitive Bento layouts.
- Endless rounded rectangles.
- Excessive glassmorphism.
- Random gradients.
- Oversized decorative blobs.
- Generic icon-and-card combinations.
- Excessive colored pills.
- Tiny technical metadata everywhere.
- Generic AI-generated illustrations.
- Repeated headline/metadata/body/card structures.
- Overuse of three-column layouts.
- Decorative charts that communicate nothing.
- Animations added merely to make the page appear sophisticated.

A strong design is not created by adding more decoration.

It is created by controlling visual hierarchy, composition, rhythm, contrast, typography, and the relationship between content and visual form.

## 2.3 Establish a clear visual signature

Develop a recognizable WORLD SIGNAL design identity.

Explore a coherent system involving:

- A distinctive masthead.
- A recognizable publication mark.
- A strong display typography system.
- A disciplined editorial grid.
- A consistent set of information-design conventions.
- Domain-specific visual accents.
- A sophisticated visual language for signals and evidence.
- A restrained but memorable motion language.

The identity should remain recognizable even if all content-specific text is removed.

Do not force a logo redesign if the existing mark is already suitable.

Instead, evaluate whether the entire publication frame, typography, spacing, section labels, and recurring visual elements work together as a recognizable system.

## 2.4 Color system

Refine the existing palette.

Preserve the warm archival-paper direction if it continues to work, but do not be afraid to improve it.

Build semantic color roles for:

- Main background.
- Secondary background.
- Elevated surfaces.
- Main text.
- Secondary text.
- Muted metadata.
- Dividers.
- Strong borders.
- Interactive states.
- Focus states.
- Domain colors.
- Evidence states.
- Confidence indicators.
- Risk and uncertainty.
- Positive, negative, and neutral changes.

Domain identity:

- World Events.
- AI Frontier.
- Economic Outlook.

The three domains should be visually distinguishable but should not create a childish rainbow interface.

Use color purposefully.

Maintain sufficient contrast in both themes.

Never rely on color alone to communicate critical meaning.

