# PART 03 — GLOBAL LAYOUT AND COMPOSITION SYSTEM

This is one of the most important parts of the entire task.

A premium frontend requires deliberate control over proportions and composition.

Do not simply place existing components into a larger container.

## 3.1 Establish a professional editorial grid

Evaluate and implement a coherent responsive grid.

Consider:

- A 12-column desktop grid.
- Flexible page margins.
- Consistent gutters.
- Strong content alignment.
- Multiple content widths.
- Carefully controlled reading measures.
- Intentional asymmetric composition.

Suggested starting points, to be validated in the actual interface:

- Large desktop page margins: approximately 48–80px.
- Standard desktop page margins: approximately 32–48px.
- Tablet margins: approximately 24–32px.
- Mobile margins: approximately 16–20px.
- Main editorial canvas: approximately 1240–1440px maximum width.
- Long-form reading column: approximately 680–800px.
- Main article sidebar: approximately 240–300px.

These values are starting references, not rigid requirements.

The correct proportions should be established through actual visual inspection.

## 3.2 Establish vertical rhythm

Every page needs intentional pacing.

Define consistent relationships between:

- Masthead and page title.
- Page title and introduction.
- Section headings and content.
- Editorial modules.
- Narrative sections.
- Data visualizations.
- Footnotes and sources.
- Page endings.

Do not allow all sections to have identical vertical spacing.

Large editorial transitions should receive more space.

Dense data sections should be compact but readable.

The page should feel composed rather than mechanically assembled.

## 3.3 Build compositional variety

Use different layout patterns according to the nature of the information.

Appropriate patterns include:

- Large editorial hero.
- Asymmetric split composition.
- Multi-column news grid.
- Numbered editorial sequence.
- Horizontal timeline.
- Vertical evidence ledger.
- Data table.
- Structured relationship diagram.
- Full-width analytical section.
- Narrow long-form reading column.
- Compact side rail.
- Typographic pull quote.
- Modular evidence callout.

Do not make all content look like cards.

Use open layouts, whitespace, borders, typography, background changes, and grid alignment to distinguish sections.

**A good page should have visual rhythm.**

It must not look like the same card repeated 20 times.

