# PART 05 — BRIEFING: DESIGN AN EXCEPTIONAL DIGITAL NEWS FRONT PAGE

Briefing is the most important entry point.

It is the page most likely to determine whether a first-time visitor continues exploring the publication.

Its visual quality must be exceptional.

## 5.1 First-screen experience

Within five seconds, a reader should understand:

- What publication they are visiting.
- What the most important current development is.
- Why it matters.
- What the publication considers important today.
- Where to go next.

Do not overwhelm the reader with metadata, controls, or technical labels.

The first screen must establish editorial authority.

## 5.2 Masthead and publication introduction

Treat the top of Briefing as a real publication masthead.

Improve:

- Brand positioning.
- Publication title.
- Edition date.
- Edition status.
- Primary navigation.
- Secondary metadata.
- Visual hierarchy.
- Responsive behavior.

The masthead should feel editorial rather than SaaS-like.

Consider a distinctive date and edition composition.

Make the publication identity memorable without making the header unnecessarily tall.

## 5.3 Lead story — build a real editorial hero

This is a priority.

The lead story must be the strongest visual element on the front page.

Do not simply place a headline next to a generic illustration.

Design a complete editorial composition consisting of:

- A carefully scaled headline.
- A strong editorial introduction.
- Clear domain identity.
- Relevant time and location context, when verified.
- A visual representation of the story.
- A meaningful summary.
- A restrained evidence/confidence presentation.
- A clear path to the full dossier.

Explore sophisticated asymmetry.

Consider a composition where typography and illustration are designed together rather than independently.

The artwork should support the story's identity.

It should not compete with the headline.

## 5.4 Story-specific visual storytelling

The previous implementation introduced `EditorialSignalArtwork`.

Treat it as a foundation, not a final solution.

Investigate whether the current visual system can become genuinely expressive.

Explore a family of custom editorial visuals:

- Distinct compositions for important stories.
- Abstract geopolitical compositions.
- Technology architecture-inspired illustrations.
- Economic structural diagrams.
- Typographic story compositions.
- Carefully constructed visual timelines.
- Evidence-inspired graphics.

Use actual structured information only.

Never fabricate a photograph, event location, geopolitical boundary, market movement, causal connection, or statistic.

If story-specific information is insufficient, use a clearly illustrative abstract visual.

The artwork must never pretend to be documentary evidence.

## 5.5 Top signals — editorial hierarchy, not repetitive cards

This area should feel like a curated editorial selection.

Create deliberate differences in visual weight.

For example:

- One strong secondary story.
- Two balanced editorial features.
- Compact supporting stories.
- Clearly separated domain sections.

Vary headline sizes, column spans, text widths, and composition according to importance.

Do not randomly vary layouts for visual novelty.

The hierarchy must communicate editorial priority.

## 5.6 Three domains — create recognizable visual identities

World Events, AI Frontier, and Economic Outlook should each have a distinct visual presentation.

Explore:

- Domain-specific graphic language.
- Elegant index compositions.
- Editorial typography.
- Real edition statistics.
- Thoughtful visual summaries.
- Meaningful links to deeper coverage.

Avoid three identical cards with an icon, a number, and a short paragraph.

## 5.7 Daily Delta

Preserve its implemented comparison engine and current editorial placement.

Make its presentation more polished.

The four categories — New, Updated, Unchanged, Omitted — must be easy to understand.

The interface should distinguish meaningful editorial change from insignificant technical changes.

Preserve all existing omission safeguards.

Never imply that omission means resolution.

## 5.8 Lower-page editorial flow

Improve the transitions between:

- Daily Delta.
- Top Signals.
- Domain overview.
- Additional Signals.
- Editorial Reading Path.

The page should feel like one intentional publication, not a stack of components.

Add visual pauses and clear section beginnings.

Do not let every module compete for attention.

